#hasheq((campaign . "v1.00.24-test-design-hotspot-remediation") (kind . "wave-review") (notes . ("additive schema compatibility: requested_execution_mode/effective_execution_mode/fallback_reason are appended per file; the characterization suite parses a frozen pre-W7 old-shape record and asserts the tolerant readers leave path/verdict/counts/output semantics unchanged, so old evidence remains readable and no existing field was renamed or repurposed" "truthful counters: a grouped request whose execution actually falls back to subprocess is reported under subprocess with the named fallback reason and is never counted as grouped; effective-mode counters in the artifact (grouped 6/12) match the effective_execution_mode fields exactly, and the repeated-stability block shows the classification is deterministic across repeats" "fallback naming stability: fallback_reason values come from a fixed vocabulary (missing-module-plus-test-form, zero-parsed-output, declared-mutation, declared-process-isolation, declared-timeout, declared-global-mutation, unsafe-classified) and are null when no fallback occurred; the suite pins these exact strings so renames would fail the test" "process-global leakage: the fixture matrix proves CWD, environment, parameters, and custodian-owned threads/ports are restored between grouped files per the existing runner contract; undeclared env mutation stays undetected-but-contained (parity holds), while declared mutation/isolation/exit/global-mutation declarations route the file to subprocess with a named reason instead of faking parity" "exception-boundary counts: the only parity nuance is parsed counts for the uncaught-exception fixture (subprocess parses 1 failing check, grouped records the file-level exception with 0 parsed checks); verdict failed and exit code 1 are identical on both planes, and the difference is documented in the report and artifact rather than hidden — verdicts may not differ, counts may, so this is within the wave invariant" "